  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This dataset shows early but real scholarly uptake: it has 2 citations, 5 documented downstream reuse events, and modest usage with 18 downloads and 108 views. The record suggests that it is being used mainly in evolutionary and protist biology research, with citations appearing in eLife articles.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Published as an open Figshare dataset in 2018, this resource accompanies the study of gene family innovation, conservation, and loss on the animal stem lineage. It was created by Daniel Richter, Parinaz Fozouni, Michael Eisen, and Nicole King, and the metadata show that it is openly accessible and linked to the related journal article.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest impact signal is reuse rather than broad attention: the dataset has 5 documented downstream reuse events and 4 formal data citations recorded through Crossref, including citation in the original eLife article and later references in subsequent publications. That pattern indicates the data have continued to support related work over time, even though the citation base remains small.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The two citations captured in OpenAlex are both journal articles in eLife, and the citing landscape is closely concentrated around protist diversity and phylogeny. That fits the dataset\u2019s biological focus and suggests relevance to researchers working on choanoflagellates, genome evolution, and comparative transcriptomics.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Usage indicators are modest but consistent with a specialized research dataset: 18 downloads and 108 views. Public-discourse tracking is unavailable, and there is no recorded scite discussion in the digest, so the clearest evidence of impact comes from scholarly reuse and citation rather than wider public engagement.</p>",
